# Commit Message Convention

Use this format when writing git commits in this repository.

## Subject line

- Default format: `<component>: <lowercase imperative summary>`
- Components used in this repo: `iosapp`, `webapp`, `server`, `caserver`,
  `gitdb`, `git-replycant`
- For cross-cutting or repo-root changes, omit the component prefix
- Use imperative wording (`add`, `fix`, `remove`, `replace`, `keep`)
- Do not add a trailing period
- Do not use conventional commit prefixes (`feat:`, `chore:`, `fix:`)
- `wip` is allowed only for explicit work-in-progress checkpoints

## Body

- Optional for trivial changes
- Required for non-trivial changes
- Put one blank line between subject and body
- Explain why the change exists and key trade-offs
- Wrap lines to about 72-80 characters

## Examples

- Good: `iosapp: prioritize interactive LFS image loads`
- Good: `Derive transcoded URL host from git server config`
- Bad: `feat: add image cache`
- Bad: `webapp: Fixed timeline seek race.`
